<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.packey.bot.plist — supervise the BOT itself (no sudo). DORMANT COPY:
  nothing is loaded while this file sits in the repo root; activating it is a
  deliberate copy + bootstrap (below).

  Why: the bot is currently a nohup child of run.sh's background mode. It
  survives terminal closes, but nothing starts it at boot and nothing restarts it
  after a crash. com.packey.tunnel's watchdog covers the crash case (its tick
  restarts a dead backend via run.sh) and covers boot only at LOGIN, through that
  same agent. This plist puts the bot on the same footing as the cloudflared
  daemon: starts at login, restarts on crash, no terminal, no manual step after a
  reboot.

  Three rules that make this safe — do not 'simplify' them away:

  1. It supervises `python3 main.py` DIRECTLY, never ./run.sh. run.sh's background
     mode starts the bot with nohup and EXITS immediately; KeepAlive would see
     that exit and respawn run.sh forever, each respawn kill-stale-ing the bot
     the previous one just started. An infinite restart loop with a kill in it.

  2. KeepAlive is SuccessfulExit=false, not plain true. The bot exits 0 on SIGINT
     (aiogram's graceful shutdown saves state first), so a manual restart does not
     look like a crash. Plain true would treat every clean exit as a failure and
     respawn over the operator's own restart.

  3. Once ACTIVE, restart the bot with `launchctl kickstart -k`, not ./run.sh.
     A manual ./run.sh SIGINTs the supervised instance (clean exit 0, no respawn —
     rule 2 holds) and then nohups a child launchd does not track: supervision
     silently desyncs until the next load or reboot.

  Also note, if you activate this:
  * Retire com.packey.tunnel (bootout below). In funnel mode its only live job is
    the bot watchdog this replaces; keeping both means two managers racing on one
    bot. run.sh's single-instance guard makes the overlap safe, not tidy.
  * ./run.sh's status mode greps the console log (~/Library/Logs/packey/bot.out)
    for the console-error count; this
    agent logs to its own files below, so that one status line will not reflect a
    launchd-run bot's console errors. Everything else in the block still reads
    the live process and store.
  * Starts at LOGIN (LaunchAgent), not before login. A root LaunchDaemon was
    rejected on purpose: it would run the bot as root and take ownership of the
    0600 data files and tracker.log.
  * PATH carries Homebrew's bin because the barcode reader (zbarimg) is a brew
    binary and launchd agents inherit a minimal PATH (same gotcha
    com.packey.tunnel.plist documents for cloudflared).

  Install:  cp com.packey.bot.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
            launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.packey.bot.plist
  Run now:  launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.packey.bot
  Remove:   launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.packey.bot.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>UMask</key>
  <integer>63</integer>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.packey.bot</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
    <string>main.py</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/fredp/glm-tracker</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/fredp/Library/Logs/packey/packey_bot_launchd.out</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/fredp/Library/Logs/packey/packey_bot_launchd.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>